
  <sn><md>### Exchange Acidity

**Definition:**
Exchange acidity is the portion of soil acidity caused by **H⁺ and Al³⁺ ions held on the exchange sites** of clay minerals and organic matter. These ions can be replaced (exchanged) by other cations from the soil solution, such as Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, or K⁺.

**Reaction:**
$$
\text{Soil} - 2\text{H}^+ + \text{Ca}^{2+} \rightarrow \text{Soil} - \text{Ca}^{2+} + 2\text{H}^+
$$

The H⁺ on the exchange site is displaced into the soil solution by Ca²⁺, increasing active acidity.

**Sources of H⁺ and Al³⁺ on Exchange Sites:**
- **Al³⁺** is the dominant exchangeable cation in acid soils (pH &lt; 5.5). It comes from the breakdown of clay minerals.
- **H⁺** comes from:
  - Organic matter decomposition (organic acids)
  - Fertilizer reactions (nitrification of NH₄⁺)
  - Acid rain
  - Plant root H⁺ release

**Hydrolysis of Al³⁺:**
Exchangeable Al³⁺ is the main source of H⁺ in acid soils:
$$
\text{Al}^{3+} + 3\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{Al(OH)}_3 + 3\text{H}^+
$$
This releases three H⁺ ions per Al³⁺, strongly acidifying the soil.

**Relation to Other Acidity Types:**

| Type | Location | Measured by |
|---|---|---|
| **Active acidity** | H⁺ in soil solution | pH |
| **Exchange acidity** | H⁺ + Al³⁺ on exchange sites | KCl extraction (titration) |
| **Residual acidity** | H⁺ bound in organic/clay structures | Difference (potential − exchange) |
| **Potential acidity** | Exchange + residual | Buffer pH method |

**Importance:**
- **Al³⁺ toxicity:** Exchangeable Al³⁺ is the most toxic form to plant roots in acid soils.
- **Lime requirement:** Exchange acidity is neutralized by liming:
$$
\text{Soil} - 2\text{Al}^{3+} + 3\text{CaCO}_3 \rightarrow \text{Soil} - 3\text{Ca}^{2+} + 2\text{Al(OH)}_3 + 3\text{CO}_2
$$
- **pH buffering:** Exchange acidity resists pH change; more lime is needed to raise pH.
- **Nutrient availability:** High exchange acidity means low base saturation (Ca, Mg, K), reducing nutrient supply.

**Measurement:**
Exchange acidity is measured by extracting soil with **1N KCl** (which displaces H⁺ and Al³⁺) and titrating the extract with base.

**Example:**
A soil with pH 4.8 has high exchangeable Al³⁺. Treating with lime (CaCO₃) displaces Al³⁺ from exchange sites, precipitates it as Al(OH)₃, and raises pH to 6.5, improving crop growth.

**Conclusion:**
Exchange acidity is the acidity due to **H⁺ and Al³⁺ held on soil exchange sites**. It is the main component of potential acidity, a key factor in **Al³⁺ toxicity**, and determines **lime requirement** in acid soils.</md></sn>
